WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:04.509
American Horror Story has been able to bring
back many of its cast members again and again

00:00:04.509 --> 00:00:07.729
in new roles every season, without boring
viewers.

00:00:07.729 --> 00:00:08.780
Why?

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:13.129
Because the show's makeup and personality
transformations have made the actors virtually

00:00:13.129 --> 00:00:16.059
unrecognizable from one year to the next.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:20.570
Here are the best American Horror Story on-screen
transformations...so far.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:22.070
Ethel Darling

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:25.869
Kathy Bates has had many memorable roles on
American Horror Story over the years.

00:00:25.869 --> 00:00:30.000
But her character from Freak Show is perhaps
her most iconic yet.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.500
In the fourth season, Bates played Ethel Darling,
a world-weary bearded lady with such a no-nonsense

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:40.040
attitude that audiences could sometimes even
forget the facial hair was even there.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.040
Papa Legba

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.050
When you're gatekeeper to the spirit world,
you'd better look the part.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:49.570
In American Horror Story: Coven, Lance Reddick
did just that as the voodoo-inspired Papa

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:53.320
Legba and was completely convincing as the
otherworldly character.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.379
"Ha Ha Ha Ha Ha"

00:00:55.379 --> 00:00:56.990
Aileen Wuornos

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:01.650
Lily Rabe had big shoes to fill when it came
to playing real-life serial killer Aileen

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:02.650
Wuornos.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:07.439
After all, Charlize Theron won an Oscar for
her portrayal in 2003's Monster.

00:01:07.439 --> 00:01:11.850
Rabe offered a sauntering performance for
American Horror Story: Hotel, but her methodical

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.460
mannerisms are just a part of the creepiness.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:19.530
Makeup artists turned her into a shiver-inducing
dead ringer of the notorious murderer.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:20.560
Pepper

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.429
American Horror Story: Asylum featured plenty
of unique characters, but none stood out more

00:01:25.429 --> 00:01:27.200
than the diminutive Pepper.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.460
A lot of makeup went into overhauling Naomi
Grossman's appearance for the role: It took

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:36.579
two people nearly three hours to apply the
prosthetic details, fake teeth, contact lenses,

00:01:36.579 --> 00:01:38.060
and makeup each day.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.789
Grossman even shaved most of her head for
the role.

00:01:40.789 --> 00:01:42.009
Liz Taylor

00:01:42.009 --> 00:01:46.609
Denis O'Hare's transformation into a transgender
woman for American Horror Story's fifth season

00:01:46.609 --> 00:01:49.729
was thorough — and not just on the outside.

00:01:49.729 --> 00:01:53.210
Aside from the makeup and outfits, O'Hare
gave a nuanced performance that showed the

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.780
emotional impact transitioning can have.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:00.139
Liz Taylor was the heart and soul of the Hotel
Cortez, and she was simply fabulous.

00:02:00.139 --> 00:02:03.959
"I'm sorry Lady, this is a notel motel."

00:02:03.959 --> 00:02:06.899
Penny
If you didn't already know Grace Gummer is

00:02:06.899 --> 00:02:10.790
Meryl Streep's daughter, you would have had
a tough time seeing the resemblance in American

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:12.280
Horror Story: Freak Show.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:17.080
To achieve her look as "Lizard Girl," Gummer
endured daily application of an elaborate

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:20.870
puzzle of prosthetic tattoos that covered
her entire head and neck.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:25.390
Although her forked tongue was added in post-production,
she did sometimes have to wear a silicon applique

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:27.010
as well.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:28.660
Infantata

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.060
Frankenstein had nothing on Infantata.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:36.080
The late Ben Woolf played the miniature monstrosity
in season 1's Murder House, and the character

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:41.190
was stunning as the reanimated remnants of
the murdered Montgomery child who's also ravenous

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:42.190
for blood.

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:46.980
To capture the unsettling look, Woolf had
to submit to one heck of a makeup routine.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.490
Twisty the Clown

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:52.280
It took a hefty amount of special effects
talent to bring Twisty the Clown to life for

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.380
American Horror Story: Freak Show.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:59.080
In case you've somehow managed to erase the
image of his origins from your nightmares,

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:04.210
Twisty shot off his jaw with a shotgun, leaving
him horribly disfigured beneath his mask.

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:08.380
No amount of makeup could make actor John
Carroll Lynch really look that way, though.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:12.770
So crew members would paint his face green
and cover it with motion capture sensors for

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:15.300
the chilling CGI effects.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.710
Tate Langdon

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:20.620
Actor Evan Peters' character in Murder House
didn't usually look all that different from

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:22.380
Evan Peters in real life.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:26.500
But when Tate applied skull makeup before
embarking on his horrific killing spree, it

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:31.100
was thoroughly terrifying and became an instant
favorite for cinch Halloween costuming.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.810
Hypodermic Sally

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:36.880
Sarah Paulson was certainly no newcomer to
the American Horror Story franchise when she

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:40.240
suited up as a drug-addled ghost to haunt
the Hotel Cortez.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:45.030
Although she also played those memorable conjoined
twins Bette and Dot in Freak Show, she's almost

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:58.340
more unrecognizable as Hypodermic Sally with
her frazzled hair and ghoulish makeup,

00:03:58.340 --> 00:03:59.340
Myrtle Snow

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:04.040
Frances Conroy's memorable turn as Myrtle
Snow was an obvious tribute to fashionista

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:08.740
Grace Coddington in Coven and was a brutally
stoic — but honest — witch who didn't

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:11.230
even flinch when burned at the stake.

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:15.720
Those who remembered Conroy best for her prior
TV work as Ruth on Six Feet Under probably

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.969
wouldn't even recognize her beneath all that
hair and flair.

00:04:18.969 --> 00:04:20.489
Edward Mordrake

00:04:20.489 --> 00:04:25.039
Weird facial hair is nothing new for actor
Wes Bentley: he'd already done it in 2012's

00:04:25.039 --> 00:04:29.439
The Hunger Games before he joined the crazy
crew of American Horror Story: Freak Show.

00:04:29.439 --> 00:04:34.110
But it wasn't just his next-level facial hair
that made his take on Edward Mordrake so memorable.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:39.230
The 19th Century Brit character also sported
an extra face on the back of his head.

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:40.330
Thanks for watching!

